from ./Conditions import Conditions
from ./HtmlOutput import HtmlOutput
from ./Layout import Layout
from ./Modules import Modules
from ./OutputSize import OutputSize
from ./Values import Values

export anchor Compositions:
    description:
        Compositions are how you arrange components to create a design.

    purpose:
        Where a component declares how something looks, a composition declares
        what is on the canvas and where. A composition is a tree of component
        instances. Each instance is drawn with its component's styling, and
        its position in the tree decides what it sits inside of.

        A composition is the unit that gets rendered. Each one becomes a single
        output, whether that is an HTML/CSS page, an SVG or a PNG.

    keywordIdentifier: composition

    keyword:
        Compositions are declared with the ${this.keywordIdentifier} keyword.

    identifier:
        Following the keyword is the name of the composition.

    braces:
        Compositions use brace syntax rather than indentation. The body of the
        composition, and the body of every component instance inside it, is
        enclosed in `\ { \` and `\ } \`.

        Braces suit compositions because, unlike components, they nest deeply.
        Explicit delimiters keep the tree readable, and keep it unambiguous,
        however deep it goes and however it is indented. Indentation inside
        braces has no meaning; it is only there to make the tree easy to
        read.

    instances:
        A component instance is the name of a component followed by a body in
        curly braces: `\ MyButton { } \`. The name must refer to a component
        declared in the same file or imported into it, as described in
        @{Modules}.
        The same component may be instanced any number of times. A
        composition can be instanced in the same way, as described under
        reuse.

    body:
        The body of an instance may hold any mix of the following:

        - text
            A quoted string, such as `"Click Me"`, is text content drawn inside
            the instance, styled by its component's typography properties.
        - attributes
            A `name: value` pair, such as `alt: Company logo`, sets an
            attribute on that one instance.
        - children
            Further component instances, nested inside this one.

        Entries are separated by commas or by line breaks, so a short body can
        sit on one line and a longer one can be spread across several. How
        commas inside values are handled is described in @{Values}.

    text:
        A body may hold any number of quoted texts, mixed freely with
        children. Text and children are placed in the order they are written,
        so text can come before, between or after child instances.

        Text is drawn with the typography of the instance's own component, and
        never with that of any instance around it, since properties do not
        cascade. Each quoted text sits on one line of source; to break text
        onto separate lines, use separate child instances. Two quoted texts
        written next to each other are drawn as one run, with nothing added
        between them. How spaces in text are collapsed, and where lines break,
        is set out in @{Layout}.

    attributes:
        Attributes carry information that is not styling, such as the image
        to show or a link's target. Each attribute becomes an HTML attribute
        on the instance's element, as described in @{HtmlOutput}. Only the
        `src` attribute affects the SVG and PNG outputs, where it names the
        image drawn by an instance whose component has the htmlTag `img`.

        Attribute names are lowercase, and may contain digits and hyphens,
        such as `href`, `alt` or `aria-label`. The value is everything after
        the colon up to the end of the entry. It may be quoted, in which case
        it can contain commas without escaping.

        Styling is never set on an instance. An attribute named after a
        component property, such as `color`, is an error, as are the names
        `class` and `style`. An instance that should look different should use
        a different component, usually one that extends the original, or set
        an attribute that its component declares.

        An attribute the component declares, as described in @{Conditions},
        does not set styling directly. It picks which of the component's
        `when` blocks apply. Its value must be one of the allowed values.

    variants:
        Emul has two ways to make a variant. A variant can be a component that
        extends another, such as `SecondaryButton extends MyButton`, chosen by
        instancing it by name. Or it can be a value of an attribute the
        component declares, chosen by setting that attribute on the instance,
        as in `\ Button { size: small } \`, as described in @{Conditions}.

    children:
        Children are laid out by their parent, using the parent component's
        layout properties, such as display, flexDirection and gap. They are
        placed in the order they are written, which is also the order in which
        they are drawn: later children are drawn above earlier ones, unless
        zIndex says otherwise.

    root:
        The body of the composition itself is the root of the tree. It holds
        component instances only, with no text or attributes, and it has no
        styling of its own.

    reuse:
        A composition can be reused as a complex component. A composition
        declared in the same file, or imported into it as described in
        @{Modules}, is instanced by name like a component:
        `\ Card { } \`. To share a composition between files, export it from
        the file that declares it and import it where it is used.

        A composition instance draws the instances in the root of the
        referenced composition in its own place, in the order they are
        written there. They become children of the instance's parent, or of
        the root, and are laid out by that parent like any other children.
        Each instance is a separate copy of the tree, and the composition
        adds no element or styling of its own, since its root has none.

        The body of a composition instance must be empty. It can't hold text,
        attributes or children, because a composition has no place to put
        them. A part of a design that changes from use to use should be built
        from shared components instead.

        A composition can't contain itself, either directly or through other
        compositions that it instances. Such a cycle is an error.

        A composition that is instanced is not rendered on its own unless it
        is also exported from the entry file.

    example:
        ```emul
        \\\
        component Logo:
            htmlTag: img
            width: 120px
            height: 32px
            objectFit: contain

        component Header:
            display: flex
            alignItems: center
            gap: 12px
            padding: 16px 24px

        component Tagline:
            fontSize: 14px
            color: #52525b

        composition Landing {
            Header {
                Logo { src: ./images/logo.png, alt: "Acme, Inc." }
                Tagline { "Designs that ", "render everywhere" }
            }
        }
        \\\
        ```

        Here `Header` is the single child of the root. It holds a `Logo`,
        drawn as the image at `./images/logo.png`, and a `Tagline` with two
        quoted texts drawn as the single run "Designs that render
        everywhere". The `alt` value is quoted, so its comma needs no escape.

    size:
        A composition's root fills the canvas, whose size is set in the config
        and on the command line, as described in @{OutputSize}.
